# Linux - Research changing file permissions

## Questions: 

1. What command changes file permissions?
2. To change permissions on a file what must the end user be? (2 answers)
3. Give examples of some different ways/syntaxes to set permissions on a new file (named testfile.txt) to:
    1. Set User to read, Group to read + write + execute, and Other to read and write only
    2.   Add execute permissions (to all entities)
    3.   Take write permissions away from Group
    4.   Use numeric values to give read + write access to User, read access to Group, and no access to Other.

# Answers 

1. The command `chmod` changes file permissions. [Reference Link](https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/html_node/chmod-invocation.html)
2. To change permissions on a filethe user must be:
   * The owner of the file.
   * The root user (superuser) `sudo`. [Reference Link](https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/html_node/chmod-invocation.html)
3. Assuming I'm a super user: 
   1. `sudo chmod 476 testfile.txt`. [Reference Link](https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/html_node/Symbolic-Modes.html)
   2. `sudo chmod 111 testfile.txt`. [Reference Link](https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/html_node/Symbolic-Modes.html)
   3. `sudo chmod g-w testfile.txt`. [Reference Link](https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/html_node/Symbolic-Modes.html)
   4. `sudo chmod 640 testfile.txt`. [Reference Link](https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/html_node/Numeric-Modes.html) 